/E (Growing Phase: In this phase, the transaction collects all the locks it needs to access data. It is not allowed to release any lock during this time.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
115 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 88 0 R
/E (Shrinking Phase: The shrinking phase begins when the transaction releases its first lock. During this phase, it may release locks, but it cannot acquire or upgrade any new lock.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
116 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 88 0 R
/E (Lock Point: The lock point is the exact moment when a transaction obtains its final lock. After the lock point, it cannot acquire any additional locks, although it may continue its operations before releasing locks.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
117 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 88 0 R
/E (Upgrading a Lock: This means converting a shared lock \(S\) into an exclusive lock \(X\). It occurs when a transaction first reads data but later needs to modify it. The upgrade is only possible during the Growing Phase, when the transaction is still acquiring locks. Additionally, the upgrade is allowed only if no other transaction holds a shared lock on that data item.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
118 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 88 0 R
/E (Downgrading a Lock: This means converting an exclusive lock \(X\) into a shared lock \(S\). It happens when a transaction finishes modifying data and only needs to read it afterward. The downgrade must occur during the Shrinking Phase, when the transaction is releasing locks. Since this reduces restrictions, it is generally allowed.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
